A bare URL the renderer has to find for itself: \e[38;2;129;162;190mhttps://example.com/plain\e[0m and one
in angle brackets \e[38;2;129;162;190mhttps://example.com/bracketed\e[0m, which are not the same thing.

Entities are resolved in prose — AT&T becomes AT&T — but left alone inside code, where \e[38;2;192;128;80m&amp;\e[0m and \e[38;2;192;128;80m&lt;div&gt;\e[0m stand as they are written.

